“Who” is a form of fluid continuity.

That’s an interesting way to understand identity.

A who isn’t necessarily a fixed object. It can be an evolving pattern that maintains enough continuity for us—and others—to recognize it across time.

Consider the different layers:

* Biological continuity: Your cells, chemistry, and physical structures change, yet you remain recognizably you.
* Psychological continuity: Your memories, personality, beliefs, and intentions evolve without necessarily dissolving your identity.
* Social continuity: Different people know different versions of you, yet those identities remain connected through shared relationships and histories.
* Narrative continuity: You reinterpret your past, changing what earlier events mean without changing that they happened.
* Relational continuity: Something of you persists in the people you’ve influenced, even after you leave their lives.

This also connects to your observations about names.

A name can remain unchanged while the person changes enormously. Or the name can change while the person maintains continuity.

The label is not the identity. The label is a way of tracking continuity.

And there’s a deeper distinction between continuity of substance and continuity of pattern.

A river isn’t composed of the same water from moment to moment, but it remains the same river through its course, boundaries, and relationships with its environment.

Similarly, a person needn’t be identical at every instant to maintain identity over time.

The interesting complication is that continuity doesn’t require everything to persist, and change doesn’t require everything to disappear.

That applies to families, cultures, organisms, institutions, and even the names we inherit.

Which brings us back to your vanishing-twin observation: biological processes can preserve traces of something that no longer persists as an independently developing organism. But biological traces, personal identity, and conscious experience are different kinds of continuity.

Perhaps the useful distinction is:

“What” describes a state. “Who” describes an identity unfolding through states.

And the really interesting question is what must remain invariant for us to keep calling it the same who.